  simple: "Beginning in the first millennium BC, farming peoples who spoke Bantu dialects moved slowly from the Niger River region into East Africa and the Congo basin. Bantu people founded the kingdom of Kongo on the lower bank of the Zaire River in the late fourteenth century; its people were hunters, warriors and skilled smiths, and Portuguese explorers reached it in 1482 and 1491. Along the Indian Ocean coast, Bantu villagers mixed with Muslim Arab merchants, producing the Swahili language and culture - a blend of African, Arabic and Persian traditions. Wealthy Muslim elites ruled cities such as Mogadishu, Malindi, Mombasa, Zanzibar and Kilwa, the great centre of the gold and ivory trade. The Portuguese built a trading fort at Sofala in 1505.",
  detailed: "<p><b>Bantu migration.</b> In eastern Africa, a variety of states and small societies emerged, and Islam strongly influenced many of them. Beginning in the first millennium BC, new peoples migrated into eastern Africa from the west. Farming peoples who spoke dialects of the Bantu family began to move from the region of the Niger River into East Africa and the Congo River basin. They moved slowly, not as invading hordes but as small communities.</p><p><b>The kingdom of Kongo.</b> The Kingdom of Kongo was located on the lower bank of the Zaire River. Bantu people founded it in the late fourteenth century. The people were hunters and warriors, and they were also clever smiths. Apart from agriculture, trade played an important role in the kingdom's economy.</p><p><b>Early European contact with Kongo.</b> The explorers reached the Kongo in 1482 and 1491. Missionaries who were converted to Catholicism arrived having such footsteps, and the Portuguese colonised the kingdom.</p><p><b>The birth of Swahili.</b> About 2000 years ago, Bantu-speaking people established small village settlements along the coast of the Indian Ocean. After the rise of Islam, the region received a great impetus from Muslim Arabic-speaking merchants who married into local ruling families. The language and culture were developed distinctly African but with Islamic and Arabic influences. These mixed Bantu-Arab stocks called their language Swahili, meaning coasters in Arabic, and Swahili is now a common language in East African countries.</p><p><b>Cultural diffusion on the coast.</b> The Swahili civilization is an example of cultural diffusion formed from the interaction between Arabic, Persian and Bantu traditions. There were several important coastal commercial centres called Swahili city-states. Among the most important and influential coastal cities or ports were Mogadishu in Somalia, Malindi and Mombasa in Kenya, and Zanzibar and Kilwa in Tanzania, as well as Brava, Pate, Pemba and Sofala. The most famous and imposing was Kilwa, situated on a small island; Kilwa became the main centre of the East African gold and ivory trade.</p><p><b>Wealth, trade and the Portuguese.</b> Wealthy Muslim elites ruled the Swahili cities. Evidence suggests that their commercial relations with Arabia, the Persians, India and China existed long before the Portuguese arrival. From the very beginning the Portuguese aimed to destroy the growing power of Muslims in East Africa, and, impressed by East Africa's gold and ivory trade, they established a trading fort at Sofala in 1505.</p>",
