  simple: "People north and south of the Sahara had long contact, and trade became better organised after the camel was introduced. The trans-Saharan caravan trade began regularly in the fourth century. Two main routes linked west and west-central Africa with north-central and north-west Africa; another crossed the central Sahara to Kanem, and a sub-branch linked West Africa with Egypt. The most important goods were gold from West Africa and salt from north-west Africa. After the Arabs appeared in the seventh century AD, enslaved people also became an important trade good. The trade made Ghana, Mali and Songhai powerful, spread Islam in West Africa, and connected Africa with the outside world, including the later trans-Atlantic slave trade.",
  detailed: "<p><b>Intra- and inter-continental relations.</b> Intercontinental relations occur between two or more continents, while intracontinental relations occur within a continent. People who lived north and south of the Sahara Desert had a long history of irregular contact before they developed the trans-Saharan trade route. Trade between these peoples became better organised after the introduction of the camel.</p><p><b>When and how the trade began.</b> The trans-Saharan caravan trade began regularly beginning in the fourth century, as an expanded version of the pre-existing intra- and inter-regional trade among peoples of various regions in Africa. We do not know exactly when the trans-Saharan trade began.</p><p><b>The routes.</b> Two important trade routes connected west and west-central Africa with north-central and north-west Africa, and they linked the major ancient and medieval states of West Africa. The other route crossed the central Sahara and connected North Africa with the kingdom of Kanem, while a sub-branch linked West Africa and Egypt. The trade routes across the desert also connected Africa with the outside world.</p><p><b>Goods and people.</b> The trans-Saharan trade network played a key role in the movement of people, materials and ideas. The important traded items of the trans-Saharan trade were gold from the regions of West Africa and salt mined in north-west Africa. In addition, enslaved people became important trade goods after the emergence of the Arabs in the seventh century AD.</p><p><b>Effects on states and religion.</b> The trans-Saharan trade contributed to developing strong economic and political systems on both sides of the Sahara. For example, the great West African empires of Ghana, Mali and Songhai became powerful because they could control the trans-Saharan trade route. In addition, the trade facilitated the spread of Islam and Islamization in West and West-Central Africa.</p><p><b>Contacts with the outside world.</b> Due to the accessibility of the East African coast, there was widespread interaction between it and people from the outside world. The early contacts were mainly with Arabs, Greeks, Persians, Romans, Chinese, Indians, Spain and Portuguese. Since the mid-fifteenth century the turn of global events brought Africa closer to the rest of the world, and Europeans, especially Portugal and Spain, were determined to dominate international trade. The outcome of Afro-European contact during the exploration era was the opening of the trans-Atlantic slave trade.</p>",
